---
// source: df0ae511359a
import FigureTable from '../../components/FigureTable.astro';
import CaseLink from '../../components/CaseLink.astro';
import { euroRange, fixedTerms, spanLabel } from '../../content/examples';
import type { Lang } from '../../i18n/languages';

interface Props {
  lang: Lang;
}
const { lang } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="fc-indemnizacion">12 days per year, except for replacement and training contracts</h3>
  <p>
    When a fixed-term contract for production circumstances (circunstancias de la producción) ends,
    you are entitled to severance pay of 12 days’ pay per year, in proportion to the days worked
    (art. 49.1.c ET). If the contract started before 2015, it is fewer days: 8 if it started in 2011
    or earlier, 9 in 2012, 10 in 2013 and 11 in 2014 (transitional provision 8.ª ET). Contracts
    signed before 4 March 2001 carry no such severance pay (transitional provision 8.ª.2 ET).
  </p>
  <p>
    Contracts to replace another person (sustitución) and training contracts (formativos) carry no
    severance pay (art. 49.1.c ET). That is why the calculator asks you the type of contract right
    after the reason.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>49.1.c</span> ET · DT <span>8.ª</span>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="fc-preaviso">Notice for contracts of more than a year</h3>
  <p>
    If the contract lasted more than a year, as can happen with a replacement contract, the employer
    has to give you at least 15 days’ notice of its end (art. 49.1.c ET). The review counts the days
    of that notice you were not given as still to be paid. Each day is worth between your monthly
    pay / 30 and your annual pay / 365, depending on how the employer counts it.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>49.1.c</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="fc-ejemplos">Examples: 3, 6 and 12 months</h3>
  <p>
    A contract for production circumstances lasts at most 6 months, or up to a year if your sector’s
    collective agreement extends it (art. 15.2 ET).
  </p>
  <FigureTable id="fc-tabla">
    <Fragment slot="caption">
      Contracts for production circumstances ending on 30 September 2026, with 1.500&nbsp;€ gross a
      month and two extra payments of 1.500&nbsp;€.
    </Fragment>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Length</th>
        <th scope="col">Severance pay</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {fixedTerms.map(({ months, limit, severance }) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            {spanLabel(months, lang, 'months')}
            {limit === 'sector_agreement' && (
              <span class="figures-note">
                only if your sector’s collective agreement extends it
              </span>
            )}
          </th>
          <td>{severance ? euroRange(severance, lang) : '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </FigureTable>
  <p>
    Each severance payment is the days of the contract × 12 / 365 days’ pay, at 21.000&nbsp;€ / 365
    each. The final settlement also adds the last month’s pay, holiday and extra payments, which the
    calculator reviews with your details.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fixed-term contract that goes beyond its maximum length may in fact be a permanent one (art.
    15.4 ET), and its end would then be a dismissal. Only contracts for a specific project or
    service (obra o servicio) signed before 31 December 2021 could last longer (transitional
    provision 3.ª of Real Decreto-ley 32/2021).
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>15</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="fc-baja">If the contract ends while you are on sick leave</h3>
  <p>
    The review calculates the final settlement with the dates, pay and holiday days you enter. It
    does not take into account the sick leave or what happens to its benefit when the contract ends:
    we do not check that.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="fc-paro">And unemployment benefit</h3>
  <p>
    The end of a fixed-term contract is a legal situation of unemployment, unless you were the one
    who ended it (art. 267.1.a.6.º LGSS). How long the benefit lasts depends on the days of
    contributions in the last 6 years: you will find it in{' '}
    <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_duration" />.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>267</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>
